<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Plauen</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Plauen is a city in the Vogtland region of south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-elster">White Elster</a>. From the 13th century it was the seat of a branch of the advocates (Vögte) of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weida">Weida</a>, and it later passed to Saxony. In the 19th century it became famous for machine-made Plauen lace, and in October 1989 it saw one of the first mass demonstrations of the peaceful revolution in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-germany">East Germany</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/plauen.jpg" alt="Plauen" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tilman2007 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Plauen,_Altes_und_Neues_Rathaus,_004.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Plauen lies on the upper reaches of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-elster">White Elster</a>, a tributary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saale">Saale</a>, in the Central Vogtlandian Hill Country. It is the largest city of the Saxon Vogtland and the south-westernmost of a string of cities that runs through Zwickau, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chemnitz">Chemnitz</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freiberg">Freiberg</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a>. It is near the borders with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czech-republic">Czech Republic</a>. The Vogtlandian dialect spoken there is a variety of East Franconian with Saxon influences, and the names of the city and of many of its districts are of Slavic origin.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polabian-slavs">Polabian Slavs</a> founded Plauen in the 12th century as Plawe. It passed to the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> in 1327 and was captured in 1384 by Lippold von Bredow, Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a>. In 1466 it passed to Albertine Saxony and in 1569 to the Electorate of Saxony. It became part of the Kingdom of Saxony in 1806, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a>, and of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a> in 1871.</p><p>In the late 19th century the town became a centre of textile manufacture, specialising in chemical lace, called Plauen lace. Around 1910 it was an industrial boomtown, with 121,000 inhabitants at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> and 128,000 in 1912, a peak from which the population has shrunk dramatically since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. Plauen's Jewish community dated from the early 14th century and numbered several hundred between the wars. Its synagogue, built in 1930 in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bauhaus">Bauhaus</a> style, was demolished in 1938 during Kristallnacht, and most of the Jewish population left or were killed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holocaust">Holocaust</a>.</p><p>In the 1930s Plauen hosted the first chapter of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazi-party">Nazi Party</a> outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>. During the war the Nazis ran a prison there and three subcamps of the Flossenbürg concentration camp, where five hundred women, mostly Polish, and fifty men were held as forced labour. American troops occupied the city on 16 April 1945 and left it to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> on 1 July. As part of the Soviet occupation zone and then of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-germany">German Democratic Republic</a>, it held a large Red Army garrison. The first mass demonstration against the communist regime began there on 7 October 1989, the start of a series across the country that led to the reunification of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in 1990.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Plauen has a population of around 65,000 and is the seat of the Vogtland District. It and the surrounding Vogtland are known as the historic centre of the German embroidery and lace industry, and the products of the region are protected under the label Plauener Spitze. Its sights include the Old City Hall, the Johanniskirche, the Embroidery Machine Museum, the Fabrik der Fäden museum of textiles and lace, and the Elster Viaduct, the second-largest brick bridge in the world after the Göltzsch Viaduct.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Plauen” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From about 1209 a branch ruled <b>Plauen</b>, and the whole region came to be called the Vogtland after their title.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-plauen">Lordship of Plauen</a></li><li><blockquote>The Reuss lords descended from the Vögte (advocates) of Weida, Gera and <b>Plauen</b>, who ruled the Vogtland from the 12th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-reuss-gera">Principality of Reuss-Gera</a></li><li><blockquote>The counts, descended from the medieval Vögte of Weida, Gera and <b>Plauen</b>, held small territories in eastern Thuringia.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-reuss-schleiz">Principality of Reuss-Schleiz</a></li></ul></section>
